Q: Vue.js watcher not detecting changes to nested object property A: <p>The <code>user</code> watcher is <a href="https://vuejs.org/guide/essentials/watchers.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">shallow by default</a>. It only fires when the <code>user</code> object itself is reassigned, so mutating a nested property like <code>user.name</code> won't trigger it.</p>
<p>To catch nested mutations, use <code>deep: true</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>watch: {
  user: {
    handler(newValue) {
      console.log('User changed:', newValue)
      this.saveUser()
    },
    deep: true
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But be careful with large objects, since <code>deep: true</code> has to recursively traverse every property. If you only care about one specific field, I'd use a dot-delimited string key instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>watch: {
  'user.name'(newName) {
    this.saveUser()
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you really need to know when any part of <code>user</code> changes, the <code>deep</code> option is the right way to go.</p>

Q: How to center selected text in PDFView? A: <p>You were kind of on the right track with the offset, but <code>page.bounds(for: .bleedBox).height / 2.0</code> is half of the PDF's page itself, it's not half of the visible screen. In other words that value uses page coordinates, not viewport coordinates. That's probably why you got the wrong offset.</p>
<p>Try this instead for your button, it should look like the GIF below:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>
Button(&quot;Center Selection&quot;)
{
    if let pdfView = self.pdfView,
       let selection = pdfView.currentSelection,
       let page = selection.pages.first
    {
        let bounds = selection.bounds(for: page)


        let topLeftPage = pdfView.convert(CGPoint(x: 0, y: 0), to: page)
        let centerPage = pdfView.convert(CGPoint(x: pdfView.bounds.midX, y: pdfView.bounds.midY), to: page)

        // offsetX is technically not needed, unless your user is going to zoom in or horizontally scroll the PDF
        let offsetX = topLeftPage.x - centerPage.x
        let offsetY = topLeftPage.y - centerPage.y


        let destinationPoint = CGPoint(
            x: bounds.midX + offsetX,
            y: bounds.midY + offsetY
        )

        let destination = PDFDestination(page: page, at: destinationPoint)

        UIView.animate(withDuration: 0.3)
        {
            pdfView.go(to: destination)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
